  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nTonight:\n\nVertically stacked low system is moving toward Hudson's Bay this\nafternoon, with some wrap around moisture maintaining scattered\nto broken skies across the Northland today. Cannot completely \nrule out a passing light shower or sprinkles, but chances are \ntoo low to mention at any given location. For tonight, high \npressure arrives from the west, and daytime, breezy winds will \ndiminish. With clearing skies and light winds, temperatures will\ncool into the upper 30s for most locations north of the Iron \nRange and across the Arrowhead. Low to mid 40s are expected \nelsewhere, with upper 40s along the shore line. With this \ncooling, frost is possible across portions of the area, with the\nmost likely area for these impacts being Koochiching County. A \nfrost advisory has been issued. Further south and east, there is\na better chance of fog which would prevent frost from becoming \nmore widespread. \n\nWednesday - Thursday:\n\nSurface high pressure will quickly slide to the east tomorrow,\nwith zonal flow aloft. A weak shortwave will bring a chance of \nisolated to scattered showers to the area late tomorrow \nafternoon into the overnight period. Rainfall amounts will be \nlight, maybe a tenth of an inch. Dry conditions return for\nThursday, with temperatures continuing to moderate. Highs will\nbe in the mid 60s to mid 70s. Overnight lows will also be\nwarmer, both Wednesday night and Thursday night, with\ntemperatures remaining above 40F both nights. \n\nFriday - This Weekend:\n\nAnother, more widespread, rain event is expect for Friday into\nSaturday. A system will move out of the Rockies with a surface\nlow strengthening over the central Plains and pushing toward the\nmid Mississippi River Valley. A warm front will move northward,\npushing up against dry air pushing southward from east central \nCanada. Current guidance suggest the warm front will remain to\nour south, with strong low level jet riding over the warm front,\nbringing widespread moderate to heavy rainfall to southern\nportions of the Northland, mainly Burnett county eastward. The \nWPC Day 4 Slight Risk area highlights this potential for heavy \nrainfall. The 19Z 9/14 run of the NBM rainfall amount \nprobabilities have trended southward with the chances for more\nthan 0.5\" of rainfall, with a 30-40% chance from Pine County\neastward.  \n\nDrier conditions are expected for Saturday and Sunday, under\nnorthwesterly upper level flow, with rain chances return early\nnext week.",
